    "result_text": "The acceptance suite is the entire test set, and all 44 tests pass.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented postcode-first locality derivation in `OwnerMapper.java`.\n\n**The change** \u2014 `locality(Owner)` now looks up the region by postcode range first (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099) via a new `regionFromPostcode` helper, and only falls back to the `CITY_REGION` city-to-region table when the postcode is absent, blank, non-numeric, or in no known range. Known cities with their pinned postcode still resolve to the same region; a city that shares a name is now disambiguated by its postcode.\n\n**A subtle trap worth calling out:** my first attempt declared the helper as a `default String regionFromPostcode(String)` method. MapStruct treats any visible `String \u2192 String` method on a mapper as an implicit conversion and silently applied it to *every* String property (city, firstName, etc.), nulling them all out. That surfaced not in the mapper but as a 500 `NullPointerException` in `AssignCustomerCode` (`city` was null) \u2014 breaking owner creation across the whole acceptance suite. The fix was to make the helper **`private`**, so MapStruct ignores it while `locality` can still call it. I noted this in a code comment so it isn't reintroduced.\n\nVerification: `Cp30Tests` (new, postcode-preference) and `Cp17Tests` (existing, city fallback) pass, and the full suite is green \u2014 **44 tests, 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\nNote: `src/test/resources/application.properties` was restored to its original state (I had temporarily added debug logging to capture the server stack trace and removed it).",
